# Phonetic features

## Manner of Articulation
The configuration and interaction of the ariculators when producing a sound.
On the broadest level, speech sounds can be classified into those that obstruct airflow (obstruents) and those that don't (sonorants). All obstruets and some sonorants are **consonants**.

Obstruents can be further divided into **plosives** (plo), also known as stops, **fricatives** (fri) and **affricates** (aff) which are consonants that begin as a plosive and release a fricative.

Sonorants can be further divided into **liquids** (liq), **nasalas** (nas), **approximants** (app), which all are consonants, and **vowels** (vow)

```dot
digraph {
  obstruents -> plo;
  obstruents -> fri;
  obstruents -> aff;
  sonorants -> vow;
  sonorants -> liq;
  sonorants -> nas;
  sonorants -> app;
}
```

## Place of Articulation
The location along the vocal tract where sound production of a consonant occurrs. Since vowels are articulated with an open vocal tract, their place cannot be easily determined. In North American English there are seven places of articulation: bilabial, labiodental, dental, alveolar, palatal, velar and glottal. Except for 'glottal' they can be paired into more general categories

```dot
digraph {
  labials -> bilabials;
  labials -> labiodentals;
  crononals -> dentals;
  crononals -> alveolars;
  dorsals -> palatals;
  dorsals -> velars;
}
```


They are independent of the manner of articulation - for example both plosives, which are obstruents, and nasals, which are sonorants, are made bilabial.

## Vowel space
The articulation of phonemes is defined along two axes. The first axis is the positioning of the tongue relative to the roof of the moth which goes from **close/high** (the tounge is pressed to the roof) via **mid** to **open/low** (the tongue is as far away from the roof as possible). More detailed descriptions include several intermediates between those three categories.

The second axis is the position of the tongue realtive to the front of the mouth. It goes from **front** (the tongue is positioned as far forward as possible without leading to a constriction that would make the sound a consonant) via **central** to **back** (the tongue is positioned as far backward as possible without leading to a constriction that would make the sound a consonant).

```dot
digraph {
  front -> central;
  central -> back
}
```

